. * FILTERS * (Please adapt according to your needs)
.  
.         * only keep participants with at least one child 
.         keep if inlist(kidsex01, 1, 2)
(10,637 observations deleted)

.         
.         * only keep particpants with valid reply on satisfaction with housewo
> rk 
.         keep if inrange(plh0174,0,10)
(8,124 observations deleted)

.         
.         * only keep particpants with partner in household
.         keep if inrange(partner,1,3)
(3,558 observations deleted)

.         *sort hid syear pid 
.         
.         * household members 
.         egen hh = count(hid), by(hid syear)

.         *g a = syear - gebjahr 
.         keep if hh == 2 
(3,783 observations deleted)

.         
.         * two different genders in the household
.         egen g = total(sex), by(hid syear)

.         keep if g == 3 
(6 observations deleted)

.         
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * generate dummy variable for whether participant's first child is fe
> male (1) or not (0)
.         gen byte d_daughter = kidsex01 == 2 

.         
.         * generate variables for gender equality in the household, as measure
> d by behaviors or outcomes 
.         *fre plh0323 plh0324 plh0174 pli0046 
.         
.         * difference in satisfaction with housework 
.         sort hid syear pid 

.         g diff = abs(plh0174 - plh0174[_n-1]) if hid == hid[_n-1] & syear == 
> syear[_n-1]
(4,560 missing values generated)

.         
.         * recode so higher is more equal 
.         g gender_eq_hh = 10 - diff 
(4,560 missing values generated)

. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. *** Having daughters leads to more gender equality in the household (as measu
> red by behaviors or outcomes). *** 
. 
.                 /* Verbal description of your model: Gender of first child is
>  exogeneous, later children may be endogeneous. Household permanence may be e
> ndogeneous, but hard to do anything about when the outcome is measured at the
>  household level. Regress the outcome on dummy variable indicating whether th
> e first child is a daughter, for parents. Include indicators for survey years
>  because of time trends and because some outcomes may be combinations of vari
> ables from different years. Cluster standard errors on individual respondent.
>   */ 
. 
.         * Regression  
. 
.         reg gender_eq_hh d_daughter i.syear, vce(cl hid)

